<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lampsacus</h1><p class="st-meta">654 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.) · capital Lampsacus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Phocaean colony on the Hellespont, famed for its wine and the cult of Priapus, where Epicurus taught and Anaxagoras died.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lampsacus was founded by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phocaea">Phocaea</a> c. 654 BCE on the Asian shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, on a site said to have been offered by a local king. Its fertile hinterland produced wine that became the city's main export.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the late 6th century BCE the tyrant Hippoclus and his son Aeantides ruled Lampsacus under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>; Aeantides married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippias-tyrant">Hippias of Athens</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i">Artaxerxes I</a> granted the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> the city's revenues for wine. Lampsacus paid large tribute in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaxagoras">Anaxagoras</a> spent his last years there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a> taught in the city before moving to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Lampsacus appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> in 196 BCE and became an ally. It passed into the Roman province of Asia in 129 BCE. The modern town of Lapseki stands on the site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lampsacus.jpg" alt="Lampsacus" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stater_Zeus_Lampsacus_CdM.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>654 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lampsacus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hippoclus, Aeantides</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Priapus</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lampsakos, Lapseki</dd></dl></aside></div>

